---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Content as CurationPolicy } from '../../docs/curation-policy.md';
import { site } from '../../site.config';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="A community-maintained calendar of computational and theoretical chemistry events, built as a static site with no tracking."
  path="/about/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      {site.name} is a community-maintained calendar of conferences, workshops and schools in
      computational and theoretical chemistry: electronic structure, molecular simulation, machine
      learning for chemistry and materials, computational materials science and computational drug
      design.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Every event is a YAML file in a public Git repository, validated automatically. The site is
      built to static files; a small Cloudflare Worker serves the few things a static file cannot,
      such as calendars for a filter you choose. Anyone can
      <a href="/submit/">add or correct an event</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      No analytics, no trackers, no third-party scripts and no external fonts. Browsing sets no
      cookies and records nothing about your visit.
    </p>
    <p>
      One exception, only if you use it: <strong>Save as my default</strong> on the event list, or
      <strong>I’m interested</strong> on an event page, sets a cookie named <code>visitor</code>
      holding a random id. With that id the site stores the filter you saved, or which events you
      marked, and when. That is all; there is no account, name or email. Pages show only how many
      people are interested, never who. The cookie is how this browser finds its filter again, and
      the filter also feeds your personal calendar link. <strong>Forget saved filter</strong>
      deletes the stored filter and turns that calendar link off; pressing
      <strong>Interested ✓</strong> again removes your mark.
    </p>

    <h2>Feeds and exports</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/events.ics">Events calendar</a> — subscribe in any calendar app.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/deadlines.ics">Deadlines calendar</a> — every open deadline.
      </li>
      <li>
        Any filter on the <a href="/">event list</a> — the “Subscribe to this filtered view” link
        under the list is a calendar of just those events that stays current.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/feed.xml">Atom feed</a> — newly added events.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/events.json">JSON</a> — the full dataset.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Renders docs/curation-policy.md, whose own `## Curation policy` heading
         carries the `#curation-policy` anchor the rest of the site links to. -->
    <CurationPolicy />

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every event page has a correction link and a report link. See the
      <a href="#curation-policy">curation policy</a> above for how reports are handled, or email
      <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Source</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={site.repoUrl} rel="noopener">
        {site.repoUrl}
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
